\title{Numerical semantics and precision in \pkg{rsimd}}
\author{Simon Potter}

\begin{document}
\SweaveOpts{concordance=FALSE}
\maketitle

<<setup, echo=FALSE>>=
options(width = 72, prompt = "R> ", continue = "+  ")
@

\pkg{rsimd} aims to give the results base R gives. This vignette describes
how it treats missing values, rounding, integer overflow, lengths and
attributes, and lists every place where it deliberately differs from base
R. The help pages have the details for each function.

<<load>>=
library(rsimd)
@

\section{NA and NaN}

R has two kinds of ``not a number'' double: \code{NaN}, the IEEE 754 result
of operations such as \code{0 / 0}, and \code{NA}, R's missing value, which
is a \code{NaN} with the special payload 1954 in its low bits. \code{is.na()}
is \code{TRUE} for both and \code{is.nan()} only for \code{NaN};
\code{simd\_is\_na()} and \code{simd\_is\_nan()} do the same:

<<na-nan>>=
v <- c(1, NA, NaN)
simd_is_na(v)
simd_is_nan(v)
@

Elementwise operations keep the payload: wherever an operand is \code{NA}
the result is \code{NA}, and \code{NaN} appears only where IEEE arithmetic
makes one. Base R usually does the same, but for \code{NaN + NA} its result
depends on the CPU.

Reductions differ from base R in one way. With \code{na.rm = FALSE},
\pkg{rsimd} returns \code{NA} if the input contains an \code{NA}, otherwise
\code{NaN} if it contains a \code{NaN}, whatever the order of the elements.
Base R's answer can depend on which comes first. A SIMD sum adds elements in
an order of its own, so ``whichever comes first'' has no meaning for it,
and \pkg{rsimd} uses a rule that does not need an order:

<<na-order>>=
c(sum(c(NA, NaN)), sum(c(NaN, NA)))
c(simd_sum(c(NA, NaN)), simd_sum(c(NaN, NA)))
@

\noindent (The base R results printed above are those of the machine that
built this document.)

\section{Removing and skipping missing values}

\code{na.rm = TRUE} removes both \code{NA} and \code{NaN}, as in base R.
\code{simd\_any\_na()}, \code{simd\_count\_na()} and \code{simd\_which\_na()}
find missing values without making a logical vector first:

<<na-rm>>=
x <- c(2.5, NA, 1, NaN, 4)
simd_sum(x, na.rm = TRUE)
simd_any_na(x)
simd_count_na(x)
simd_which_na(x)
@

Looking for missing values costs time, especially for integers, where
\code{NA} is an ordinary bit pattern that arithmetic would otherwise treat
as the number $-2^{31}$. If you know that a vector has no missing values,
pass \code{na\_check = FALSE}, or set \code{options(rsimd.na\_check =
FALSE)} for every call, and the functions skip the checks:

<<na-check>>=
clean <- c(1L, 5L, 9L)
simd_sum(clean, na_check = FALSE)
@

\begin{center}
\fbox{\parbox{0.9\textwidth}{\textbf{Warning.} \code{na\_check = FALSE} is a
promise. If the input does contain missing values the result is unspecified:
an integer \code{NA} may be counted as $-2147483648$ and a double \code{NA}
may come back as \code{NaN}. It never crashes R, but the answer is wrong.
\code{na.rm = TRUE} always looks for missing values, whatever
\code{na\_check} says. A \code{simd\_vec} made with \code{check\_na = TRUE}
gives the same speed safely, by checking once.}}
\end{center}

\section{Precision of floating-point sums}

Floating-point addition is not associative: the result of a sum depends on
the order of the additions. A SIMD sum keeps one partial sum per lane and
adds them at the end, which is faster than one running total but rounds
differently, and differently on tiers with different numbers of lanes. Base
R's \code{sum()} instead keeps one running total in \code{long double} where
the platform has one wider than double: 80 bits on x86-64 and 128 bits on
arm64 Linux, while on arm64 macOS \code{long double} is just a double.

\pkg{rsimd} offers three precision modes, set with \code{simd\_precision()}
or the option \code{rsimd.precision}:

\begin{description}
\item[\code{"fast"}] (the default) partial sums in the lanes, added at the
  end. The result depends on the tier.
\item[\code{"pairwise"}] blocks of 128 elements summed as in \code{"fast"},
  then the block sums combined pairwise, so rounding errors grow with
  $\log n$ rather than $n$.
\item[\code{"compensated"}] Neumaier's compensated summation, which carries
  the rounding error of every addition along. Its result is within about
  one rounding of the exact sum unless the sum cancels heavily, it agrees
  across tiers, and it needs no \code{long double}.
\end{description}

\noindent The modes affect \code{simd\_sum}, \code{simd\_mean},
\code{simd\_sum\_sq}, \code{simd\_sum\_abs}, \code{simd\_dot},
\code{simd\_norm}, \code{simd\_dist}, \code{simd\_cosine}, \code{simd\_var},
\code{simd\_sd}, \code{simd\_cumsum} and the denominators of
\code{simd\_softmax} and \code{simd\_log\_softmax}. Nothing else changes.

The classic example is \code{c(1e16, 1, -1e16)}, whose exact sum is 1.
Adding in order in double precision loses the 1, because $10^{16} + 1$ is
not representable and rounds to $10^{16}$. The chunk below runs on the
\code{"none"} implementation, which adds in order on every machine, so its
output is the same everywhere:

<<precision-none>>=
x <- c(1e16, 1, -1e16)
with_precision <- function(mode, expr) {
  old <- simd_precision(mode)
  on.exit(simd_precision(old))
  expr
}
modes <- c("fast", "pairwise", "compensated")
simd_with_impl("none",
  sapply(modes, function(m) with_precision(m, simd_sum(x))))
@

On a SIMD tier the result of \code{"fast"} and \code{"pairwise"} depends on
how the three elements fall into lanes: with two lanes, $10^{16}$ and
$-10^{16}$ share a lane and cancel exactly, and the 1 survives in the other.
With this tiny input the answer can be right by luck; with real data the
lanes' rounding errors are just as real, only harder to see. Here are the
three modes on the implementation in use, and base R:

<<precision-current>>=
simd_current()
sapply(modes, function(m) with_precision(m, simd_sum(x)))
sum(x)
@

\noindent Base R's result is 1 where \code{long double} is wider than
double, and 0 where it is not (arm64 macOS, or R built without
\code{long double}). Only \code{"compensated"} gives 1 everywhere.

\code{"pairwise"} costs little over \code{"fast"} and makes results
independent of how the input is split internally, though tiers can still
differ in about the last bit. \code{"compensated"} costs more; the
vignette \emph{Benchmarks} has a table of the cost. A reasonable choice is
\code{"fast"} for exploratory work and \code{"compensated"} when results
must be reproducible across machines.

\section{Integer overflow}

R's integers are 32-bit, and \code{NA\_integer\_} is the bit pattern of
$-2^{31}$, so valid values range over $\pm(2^{31} - 1)$. As in base R, a
result outside that range is \code{NA}, with one warning per call:

<<overflow>>=
big <- c(2L, 50000L)
withCallingHandlers(
  simd_mul(big, 50000L),
  warning = function(w) {
    message("warning: ", conditionMessage(w))
    invokeRestart("muffleWarning")
  }
)
@

\noindent (The warning is printed to the console, not into this
document.) The \code{\_wrap} variants (\code{simd\_add\_wrap()},
\code{simd\_sub\_wrap()}, \code{simd\_mul\_wrap()}, \code{simd\_neg\_wrap()},
\code{simd\_abs\_wrap()}) wrap around in two's complement instead, as C
unsigned arithmetic does, and never warn. A result that wraps to $-2^{31}$
reads as \code{NA}:

<<wrap>>=
simd_mul_wrap(big, 50000L)
simd_add_wrap(.Machine$integer.max, 1L)
@

Integer sums are exact. As in base R, \code{simd\_sum()} of an integer
vector returns an integer when the total fits and a double when it does not:

<<int-sum>>=
simd_sum(c(.Machine$integer.max, 1L))
class(simd_sum(1:10))
@

\section{Lengths, empty inputs and attributes}

Unary functions keep the length of their input. Binary and ternary
functions need operands of the same length, or of length one: a length-one
operand is used for every element. Base R also recycles other lengths
(with a warning when they do not divide); \pkg{rsimd} reports an error,
since unequal lengths are almost always a mistake:

<<lengths>>=
simd_add(1:4, 10L)
msg <- tryCatch(simd_add(1:4, 1:2), error = conditionMessage)
writeLines(strwrap(msg, 70))
@

\noindent \code{simd\_dot()}, \code{simd\_dist()} and \code{simd\_cosine()}
need equal lengths and do not broadcast a length-one operand.

Empty inputs give base R's results:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Call on \code{numeric(0)} & Result \\
\hline
\code{simd\_sum}, \code{simd\_sum\_sq}, \code{simd\_dot} & \code{0} (\code{0L} for integers) \\
\code{simd\_prod} & \code{1} \\
\code{simd\_mean} & \code{NaN} \\
\code{simd\_var}, \code{simd\_sd} & \code{NA} \\
\code{simd\_min}, \code{simd\_max} & \code{Inf}, \code{-Inf}, with base R's warning \\
\code{simd\_any}, \code{simd\_all} & \code{FALSE}, \code{TRUE} \\
elementwise and cumulative functions & a zero-length vector \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Results are bare vectors: names, dimensions and other attributes are
dropped, where base R's operators keep them. The exceptions are the
\code{integer64} class, which is kept, and \code{simd\_vec} operands, whose
results are \code{simd\_vec} objects again. Put attributes back yourself
when you need them:

<<attributes>>=
m <- matrix(1:4, 2)
r <- simd_add(m, 1L)
r
dim(r) <- dim(m)
r
@

\section{Differences from base R}

Every deliberate difference, with its reason and what to do about it:

{\small
\begin{longtable}{p{0.2\textwidth}p{0.35\textwidth}p{0.37\textwidth}}
\hline
Topic & Difference & Reason and workaround \\
\hline
\endhead
\hline
\endfoot
\code{NA} and \code{NaN} in reductions &
  \code{NA} whenever an \code{NA} is present, whatever the order; base R's
  answer can depend on the order. &
  A SIMD reduction has no order. Use \code{simd\_count\_na()} or
  \code{is.nan()} to tell them apart. \\
\code{NA} and \code{NaN} in elementwise operations &
  \code{NA} wherever an operand is \code{NA}; base R may give \code{NaN}
  for \code{NaN + NA}, depending on the CPU. &
  \pkg{rsimd} keeps missing values missing on every CPU. \\
Double sums &
  Accumulated in double, in the mode of \code{simd\_precision()}; base R
  uses \code{long double} where available. &
  Speed. Use \code{"compensated"} for accurate, reproducible sums. \\
\code{simd\_prod} &
  Multiplies in double; an intermediate product can overflow where base
  R's \code{long double} would not. &
  Speed. Use \code{prod()} when partial products may leave the double
  range. \\
\code{simd\_cumsum} &
  In \code{"fast"} and \code{"pairwise"} modes, can differ from base R in
  the last bits. &
  Lanes are added in a tree. \code{"compensated"} is sequential and
  identical on every tier. \\
Recycling &
  Only length-one operands are reused; other unequal lengths are an
  error. &
  Avoids silent mistakes. Use \code{rep\_len()} if recycling is
  intended. \\
Attributes &
  Names, dimensions and other attributes are dropped. &
  Keeps results simple and fast. Copy them back, as in the example
  above. \\
\code{simd\_pmin}, \code{simd\_pmax} &
  Exactly two operands. &
  Nest calls for more operands. \\
\code{simd\_round(x, digits)}, \code{simd\_log(x, base)} &
  \code{digits} and \code{base} must be single numbers. &
  Kernels take one constant. Use \code{round()} or \code{log()} for
  vectors of them. \\
Elementary functions &
  May differ from base R in the last bit. &
  The SIMD tiers use SLEEF, within 1 ULP of the exact result, or 3.5 ULP
  in fast mode (see below); the \code{"none"} tier uses the C library as
  base R does. \\
Odd functions of \code{-0} &
  \code{simd\_sin(-0)} and similar return \code{-0}; base R since 4.6.0
  returns \code{0}. &
  The IEEE 754 and C rule. Add \code{0} to turn \code{-0} into \code{0}. \\
\code{simd\_idiv}, \code{simd\_mod} on doubles &
  Always exact; base R computes in \code{long double} and can differ in
  the last bits, or warn about loss of accuracy, for large quotients. &
  Exactness. No workaround needed. \\
Complex elementary functions &
  The SIMD implementations are within 2 to 5 ULP of the exact value
  (\code{?simd\_exp}); on base R's branch cuts (such as
  \code{asin(2+0i)}) they compute base R's own branch-cut formula, within
  2 ULP of it; the \code{"none"} implementation and whole-number powers
  are identical to base R. &
  Speed. Base R's C library is within about 5 ULP itself; use
  \code{simd\_with\_impl("none", ...)} for base R's exact values. \\
Complex \code{acosh} &
  The principal value (C's \code{cacosh}); base R's \code{acosh()} has a
  negative real part in the lower half-plane and for real $z > 1$. &
  Correctness: base R computes \code{acos(z) * 1i}. Negate base R's value
  where its real part is negative to get \pkg{rsimd}'s. \\
Complex \code{prod}, \code{mean} &
  Products multiply with base R's \code{x * y} (which recovers infinities)
  in double or, in compensated mode, double-double precision; base R
  multiplies in \code{long double} without that recovery. Means skip base
  R's second pass. &
  Results with infinite or overflowing parts can differ; finite results
  differ in the last bits. Use compensated mode for accuracy. \\
\code{integer64} with a double &
  The result is double, with the warning ``integer64 coerced to double'';
  \pkg{bit64} converts the double to \code{integer64}. &
  Keeps the double's fraction. Convert with
  \code{simd\_as\_integer64()} first for integer arithmetic. \\
\code{integer64} sums &
  \code{NA} only if the exact total is out of range; \pkg{bit64} checks
  every running total. &
  Order-independent, like every \pkg{rsimd} sum. \\
\code{na\_check = FALSE} &
  No base R equivalent; results are unspecified if there are missing
  values. &
  Only for data known to be complete. \\
\end{longtable}
}

\section{64-bit integers}

\pkg{rsimd} works on the \code{integer64} vectors of the \pkg{bit64}
package: doubles whose bits hold signed 64-bit integers, with class
\code{"integer64"}. It computes on them without \pkg{bit64}, which is only
needed to create and print them; \code{simd\_as\_integer64()} creates them
too. Arithmetic is exact, and overflow gives \code{NA} with \pkg{bit64}'s
warning. The chunk runs only when \pkg{bit64} is installed:

<<integer64>>=
if (requireNamespace("bit64", quietly = TRUE)) {
  max64 <- bit64::as.integer64("9223372036854775807")
  big <- c(max64, bit64::as.integer64(c(1, -1)))
  print(simd_sum(big))
  print(suppressWarnings(sum(big)))
  y <- simd_as_integer64(c(1, 2, 3))
  print(simd_mul(y, 4L))
  print(suppressWarnings(simd_add(y, 0.5)))
} else {
  cat("bit64 is not installed\n")
}
@

\noindent The first sum shows the difference in the table above:
$2^{63} - 1 + 1 - 1$ is in range, so \code{simd\_sum()} returns it, while
\pkg{bit64}'s running total overflows at the second element. The last line
gave a double, with a warning. \code{?"rsimd-integer64"} lists the functions
that take \code{integer64} and their rules.

\section{Accuracy of elementary functions}

On the SIMD tiers the elementary functions (\code{simd\_exp()},
\code{simd\_sin()}, \code{simd\_pow()} and the rest) come from the bundled
SLEEF library. \pkg{rsimd} uses SLEEF's 1-ULP variants, and its 0.5-ULP
variants for \code{simd\_sinpi()}, \code{simd\_cospi()} and
\code{simd\_hypot()}: results are within one unit in the last place (ULP) of
the exact value, or correctly rounded for the 0.5-ULP ones. The C library
that base R uses is usually also within 1 ULP, but not always the same
ULP, so results can differ from base R and between tiers in the last bit.
The \code{"none"} tier calls the C library, as base R does.

On the \code{"neon"} tier (arm64), \code{simd\_log()} (with or without a
base), \code{simd\_log2()}, \code{simd\_cosh()}, \code{simd\_asinh()},
\code{simd\_acosh()} and \code{simd\_pow()} call the C library too, as
\code{"none"} does, because there it was measured faster than SLEEF's
two-lane vector code (on Apple cores with glibc; the list is kept only for
functions where that holds on Neoverse cores too). Their results
on \code{"neon"} are therefore base R's, bit for bit. In fast mode
\code{simd\_log()}, \code{simd\_log2()} and \code{simd\_cosh()} use
SLEEF's 3.5-ULP variants again, and the others, which have none, stay on the
C library. The wider tiers (\code{"sve"}, \code{"avx2"} and so on) use SLEEF
for all of them.

The tests compare every tier with \code{"none"} and allow 2 ULP, plus exact
checks at special values (zeros, infinities, \code{NA}, \code{NaN}, overflow
and underflow thresholds). You can measure the difference on your own
machine:

<<ulp>>=
ulp_diff <- function(a, b) {
  abs(a - b) / 2^(floor(log2(pmax(abs(a), abs(b)))) - 52)
}
x <- seq(-10, 10, length.out = 1e4)
table(ulp_diff(simd_sin(x), sin(x)))
@

\noindent The table counts the elements by how many ULP apart the two
results are. Run on the \code{"none"} tier, it would show only zeros
where the C library is the same one base R uses.

\subsection{Fast mode}

\code{simd\_math\_accuracy("fast")}, or option
\code{rsimd.math\_accuracy = "fast"}, makes the SIMD tiers use SLEEF's
3.5-ULP variants instead, which are typically 1.5 to 3 times as fast (6
times for \code{simd\_hypot()}). It affects the trigonometric, inverse
trigonometric and hyperbolic functions (not the inverse hyperbolic ones),
\code{simd\_log()}, \code{simd\_log2()}, \code{simd\_cbrt()},
\code{simd\_hypot()}, \code{simd\_atan2()} and the pi-scaled functions.
SLEEF has no faster variant of the exponentials, \code{simd\_pow()},
\code{simd\_log10()}, \code{simd\_log1p()} and the inverse hyperbolic
functions, so they, and the machine-learning helpers built on
\code{exp}, give the same results in both modes. Only the last bits of
finite results change: \code{NA} and \code{NaN}, infinities, signs of
zero, exact cases and warnings stay the same. The mode is global and
applies to every tier (\code{"none"} always uses the C library).
\code{?simd\_math\_accuracy} lists the bound of each function in both
modes.

<<fast>>=
old <- simd_math_accuracy("fast")
table(ulp_diff(simd_sin(x), sin(x)))
simd_math_accuracy(old)
@

\subsection{Exact operations and roots}

Some functions need no approximation at all and give identical results on
every tier: \code{simd\_ilogb()} (the binary exponent),
\code{simd\_scaleb()} (\code{x * 2\^{}n}, rounded once),
\code{simd\_nextafter()}, \code{simd\_next\_up()},
\code{simd\_next\_down()}, \code{simd\_remainder()} (the IEEE remainder,
whose quotient is rounded to nearest rather than down as in
\code{\%\%}) and \code{simd\_rsqrt()}, which is base R's
\code{1 / sqrt(x)}. \code{simd\_rootn(x, n)} is within 1 ULP of the real
\code{n}-th root and exact when the root is, which \code{x\^{}(1/n)} is not:

<<rootn>>=
c(1000^(1 / 3), simd_rootn(1000, 3), simd_rootn(-8, 3))
simd_remainder(c(5, 7), 3) - c(5, 7) %% 3
@

\section{Approximations}

\code{simd\_recip\_approx()} and \code{simd\_rsqrt\_approx()} trade
accuracy for speed in a different way from fast mode: they are within a
relative error of $2^{-22}$ (about single precision) of \code{1 / x} and
\code{1 / sqrt(x)} on every tier, but each tier gets there its own way,
from a hardware estimate refined by Newton steps, or by computing the
exact value where that is faster, so the results are not reproducible
across tiers. Zeros, infinities, subnormal numbers, the largest numbers
and missing values are always handled exactly.
\code{simd\_mul\_add\_approx()} is \code{x * y + z} rounded once where
the CPU has a fused multiply-add and twice elsewhere. Their help page
lists the method and bound of each tier; they do not depend on
\code{rsimd.math\_accuracy}.

<<approx>>=
x <- 10^seq(-300, 300, length.out = 1e4)
max(abs(simd_recip_approx(x) * x - 1))
max(abs(simd_rsqrt_approx(x) * sqrt(x) - 1))
@

\end{document}
